## Why This Is Asked

Interview context explanation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit as two 64-bit halves; parallel popcount masks; integer promotion.
- No loops; constant-time composition.

## Code Example

```javascript
unsigned countSetBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  auto pc64 = [](uint64_t v){
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v >> 8);
    v = v + (v >> 16);
    v = v + (v >> 32);
    return v & 0x7F;
  };
  return pc64(lo) + pc64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 256-bit types?
- Discuss trade-offs vs. using builtin popcount intrinsics.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit hacks and understanding of 8×8 transpose, a common optimization in graphics, crypto, and compression. It checks for precise, branchless manipulation and correct use of masks.

## Key Concepts
- fixed-sequence bit swaps
- 8×8 bit matrix representation
- mask constants, shifts, no loops

## Code Example
```cpp
uint64_t transpose64(uint64_t x) {
    const uint64_t m1  = 0x5555555555555555ULL;
    x = (x & m1) << 1 | (x >> 1) & m1;
    const uint64_t m2  = 0x3333333333333333ULL;
    x = (x & m2) << 2 | (x >> 2) & m2;
    const uint64_t m4  = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    x = (x & m4) << 4 | (x >> 4) & m4;
    const uint64_t m8  = 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
    x = (x & m8) << 8 | (x >> 8) & m8;
    const uint64_t m16 = 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
    x = (x & m16) << 16 | (x >> 16) & m16;
    const uint64_t m32 = 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;
    x = (x & m32) << 32 | (x >> 32) & m32;
    return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with randomized 64-bit inputs to validate correctness?
- How would you generalize to 16×16 blocks with a 256-bit input stored in four 64-bit words?

## Why This Is Asked
Transposition in bit-sliced form is a classic challenge that tests mastery of layout tricks and how data moves across a 128-bit container without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice layout and Morton-order ideas
- Multi-stage bit redistribution with masks
- Branchless, vector-style bit manipulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline for guidance
uint128 transpose8x16(uint128 x) {
  // stage 1: swap adjacent bits
  x = (x & 0x5555...5555) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555...5555);
  // stage 2: swap pairs
  x = (x & 0x3333...3333) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333...3333);
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x & 0x0F0F...0F0F) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F...0F0F);
  // stage 4: move bytes
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design branchless bit tricks for palindrome detection on 64-bit values, a common bitboard problem.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR-based bit reversal
- Branchless boolean results
- Fixed-width masking patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
function reverse64(n) {
  n = ((n >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  n = ((n >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  n = ((n >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n);
  n = ((n >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n);
  n = ((n >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n);
  n = (n >> 32n) | (n << 32n);
  return n;
}
function isPalindrome64(n) {
  return reverse64(n) === n;
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests advanced bit manipulation skills. A palindrome check requires full 128-bit reversal and a single branchless comparison. It assesses understanding of endianness, cross-lane bit operations, and how to compose a complex operation from a reusable primitive.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit bit reversal (SWAR-style steps)
- Branchless comparison via bitwise XOR
- Endian-agnostic bit ordering across two 64-bit lanes

## Code Example
```c
int isBitPalindrome128(uint128 x) {
    uint128 r = reverseBits128(x);
    uint128 d = x ^ r;
    return (d == 0) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

